  "content": {
    "id": "p63-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Solve by first taking out a common factor.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p63-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2x^3-14x^2+14x+30=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-1,\\ 3,\\ 5$",
          "worked": "$$2(x^3-7x^2+7x+15)=0.$$\nDivide by $2$ and let $Q(x)=x^3-7x^2+7x+15$. Test factors of $15$: $Q(-1)=-1-7-7+15=0$, so $x+1$ is a factor. Long division, subtracting each product row, gives\n$$\\begin{array}{rrrrr}&x^2&-8x&+15&\\\\\\hline x+1&x^3&-7x^2&+7x&+15\\\\&x^3&+x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&-8x^2&+7x&+15\\\\&&-8x^2&-8x&\\\\\\hline &&&15x&+15\\\\&&&15x&+15\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}2(x+1)(x^2-8x+15)&=0\\\\2(x+1)(x-3)(x-5)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $x=-1,\\ 3,\\ 5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p63-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$3x^3+12x^2+3x-18=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-3,\\ -2,\\ 1$",
          "worked": "$$3(x^3+4x^2+x-6)=0.$$\nDivide by $3$ and let $Q(x)=x^3+4x^2+x-6$. Test factors of $-6$: $Q(1)=1+4+1-6=0$, so $x-1$ is a factor. Long division, subtracting each product row, gives\n$$\\begin{array}{rrrrr}&x^2&+5x&+6&\\\\\\hline x-1&x^3&+4x^2&+x&-6\\\\&x^3&-x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&5x^2&+x&-6\\\\&&5x^2&-5x&\\\\\\hline &&&6x&-6\\\\&&&6x&-6\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}3(x-1)(x^2+5x+6)&=0\\\\3(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $x=-3,\\ -2,\\ 1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
  },
